dts: rk3576-armsom-sige5: wire the WiFi host wake and enable Bluetooth

The board file brings the SDIO controller up but never names what is on it,
so brcmfmac gets no out-of-band host wake: of.c sets oob_irq_supported only
when a "host-wake" interrupt is present, and bcmsdh.c registers the OOB IRQ
only when it is set. The pin was already described - wifi_wake_host, GPIO0_B0
- and nothing referenced it.

Mainline ships the wiring as an overlay,
rk3576-armsom-sige5-v1.2-wifibt.dtso, for exactly this v1.2 module. This
system builds one device tree and applies no overlays, so the same nodes go
in the board file. The wiring is correct on that basis alone.

What this does not explain is the fault that prompted it:

  brcmfmac: brcmf_sdio_bus_sleep: error while changing bus sleep state -110

seen about 3 times an hour, once followed by brcmf_sdio_txfail.
brcmf_sdio_kso_control() does its handshake with brcmf_sdiod_readb/writeb on
SBSDIO_FUNC1_SLEEPCSR whether or not an OOB interrupt exists - within sdio.c
oob_irq_supported only gates wowl_supported - so the KSO exchange runs over
SDIO either way and the mechanism is not "OOB moves the handshake off the
bus". Any effect here is indirect and unproven.

Measured after this patch: brcmf_oob_intr appears in /proc/interrupts on
GPIO0_B0 and counts up, where before there was no brcmf line at all. Error
counts over a long soak are the only thing that can decide whether the -110
is affected; do not read this patch as a fix for it until that is done.

The LPO clock the overlay also names is already reaching the module through
sdio_pwrseq's ext_clock, so only the interrupt is new.

Bluetooth comes with it, from the same overlay: uart4 goes to okay and gains
the bcm43438-bt serdev child. The kernel side needs nothing - the arm64
defconfig has BT_HCIUART_BCM, which selects BT_HCIUART_H4 and BT_BCM, and
BT_HCIUART_SERDEV defaults on with SERIAL_DEV_BUS. The controller reports
itself as BCM4362A2 and asks for brcm/BCM4362A2.hcd, which
package/brcmfmac43752-firmware installs.

This narrows the board file to v1.2 and later. Mainline keeps the same nodes
in an overlay precisely so one DTB serves every revision; building a single
device tree gives that up. Earlier boards carry an RTL8852BS, which has no
mainline driver and does not share this wiring, so they would now get reset
and wake GPIOs driven for a module that is not there. The README says v1.2 or
later is required rather than leaving that implicit.

Signed-off-by: Isaiah DeRose-Wilson <isaiahdw@gmail.com>

--- a/arch/arm64/boot/dts/rockchip/rk3576-armsom-sige5.dts
+++ b/arch/arm64/boot/dts/rockchip/rk3576-armsom-sige5.dts
@@ -7,6 +7,7 @@
 /dts-v1/;
 
 #include <dt-bindings/gpio/gpio.h>
+#include <dt-bindings/interrupt-controller/irq.h>
 #include <dt-bindings/leds/common.h>
 #include <dt-bindings/pinctrl/rockchip.h>
 #include <dt-bindings/pwm/pwm.h>
@@ -920,6 +921,8 @@ &sdhci {
 };
 
 &sdio {
+	#address-cells = <1>;
+	#size-cells = <0>;
 	bus-width = <4>;
 	cap-sdio-irq;
 	disable-wp;
@@ -934,6 +937,28 @@ &sdio {
 	vqmmc-supply = <&vcc_1v8_s3>;
 	wakeup-source;
 	status = "okay";
+
+	/*
+	 * Naming the module is what gives brcmfmac an out-of-band host wake:
+	 * of.c sets oob_irq_supported only when this "host-wake" interrupt is
+	 * present, and bcmsdh.c registers the OOB IRQ only when it is set.
+	 * Without the node the driver falls back to MMC_PM_WAKE_SDIO_IRQ.
+	 *
+	 * This is upstream's own wiring for the v1.2 module, which is the
+	 * reason for it. It is not a fix for the KSO timeouts seen on this
+	 * board - that handshake runs over SDIO function 1 either way.
+	 */
+	wifi@1 {
+		compatible = "brcm,bcm4329-fmac";
+		reg = <1>;
+		clocks = <&hym8563>;
+		clock-names = "lpo";
+		interrupt-parent = <&gpio0>;
+		interrupts = <RK_PB0 IRQ_TYPE_LEVEL_HIGH>;
+		interrupt-names = "host-wake";
+		pinctrl-names = "default";
+		pinctrl-0 = <&wifi_wake_host>;
+	};
 };
 
 &sdmmc {
@@ -977,6 +1002,22 @@ &uart4 {
 	pinctrl-0 = <&uart4m1_xfer &uart4m1_ctsn &uart4m1_rtsn>;
 	pinctrl-names = "default";
 	uart-has-rtscts;
+	status = "okay";
+
+	bluetooth {
+		compatible = "brcm,bcm43438-bt";
+		clocks = <&hym8563>;
+		clock-names = "lpo";
+		device-wakeup-gpios = <&gpio1 RK_PD7 GPIO_ACTIVE_HIGH>;
+		interrupt-parent = <&gpio0>;
+		interrupts = <RK_PB1 IRQ_TYPE_LEVEL_HIGH>;
+		interrupt-names = "host-wakeup";
+		pinctrl-names = "default";
+		pinctrl-0 = <&bt_reg_on>, <&bt_wake_host>, <&host_wake_bt>;
+		shutdown-gpios = <&gpio0 RK_PC6 GPIO_ACTIVE_HIGH>;
+		vbat-supply = <&vcc_3v3_s3>;
+		vddio-supply = <&vcc_1v8_s3>;
+	};
 };
 
 &usb_drd0_dwc3 {
